Geo Overview
Geo

!!!Introduction

The <*Geo*> library (<*PocoGeo*>, namespace <[Poco::Geo]>) works with geographic positions
on the surface of a globe: angles and latitude/longitude positions, great-circle and
rhumb-line distances and azimuths, conversions to and from local cartesian coordinates, and
circular and polygonal geofences.

Dependencies: Foundation.


!!!Classes

<[Angle]> represents a geometric angle. Angles are created with <[Angle::fromDegrees()]>,
<[Angle::fromRadians()]>, the latitude and longitude variants of those, and
<[Angle::fromXY()]>. The class provides the arithmetic and comparison operators,
normalization to the latitude, longitude and [0, 360) intervals, the sine and cosine of the
angle, and the compass direction (N, NNE, NE, ...) an angle points to.

<[LatLon]> is a position on the surface of a globe, given as a latitude in [-90, 90]
degrees and a longitude in [-180, 180] degrees. It computes the great-circle distance and
azimuth between two positions and the end position of a great-circle arc, the same three for
rhumb lines (lines of constant heading), and converts to and from cartesian coordinates
relative to a reference position. Distances are returned as the angle between the two
positions; multiply the angle in radians by the radius of the globe, for example
<[LatLon::EARTH_MEAN_RADIUS]>, to obtain a distance in meters.

<[Cartesian]> holds a pair of cartesian coordinates in meters, as used by
<[LatLon::toCartesian()]> and <[LatLon::fromCartesian()]>.

<[Geofence]> is the base class of the geofences. Its <[isInside()]> method returns true if the
given position lies inside the geofence.

<[CircularGeofence]> is a geofence around a center position with a radius in meters.

<[PolygonalGeofence]> is a geofence defined by the polygon through a sequence of positions. It
converts the polygon and the position to cartesian coordinates and tests the position with the
even-odd rule. A position exactly on a vertex of the polygon counts as outside the geofence;
the rest of the boundary is half-open.

A position outside the domain of a computation, and a position with a NaN coordinate, yield
NaN results. A geofence reports such a position as outside.


!!!Building Geo

!!CMake

The CMake option <*ENABLE_GEO*> is on by default and off with <*POCO_MINIMAL_BUILD*>, where it
must be enabled explicitly.

    cmake -B build -DPOCO_MINIMAL_BUILD=ON -DENABLE_GEO=ON
    cmake --build build

!!GNU Make

The component <*Geo*> is part of the default build.


!!!Examples

The following program tests two positions against a polygonal geofence with four vertices.

    #include "Poco/Geo/PolygonalGeofence.h"
    #include <iostream>
    #include <vector>

    using Poco::Geo::LatLon;
    using Poco::Geo::PolygonalGeofence;

    int main()
    {
        const std::vector<LatLon> polygon {
            LatLon::fromDegrees(48.205000, 16.370000),
            LatLon::fromDegrees(48.205000, 16.380000),
            LatLon::fromDegrees(48.212000, 16.380000),
            LatLon::fromDegrees(48.212000, 16.370000)
        };
        PolygonalGeofence geofence(polygon);
        LatLon inside(LatLon::fromDegrees(48.208500, 16.375000));
        LatLon outside(LatLon::fromDegrees(48.220000, 16.375000));
        std::cout << (geofence.isInside(inside) ? "inside" : "outside") << std::endl;
        std::cout << (geofence.isInside(outside) ? "inside" : "outside") << std::endl;
        return 0;
    }

The following program tests a position against a circular geofence with a radius of
500 meters.

    #include "Poco/Geo/CircularGeofence.h"
    #include <iostream>

    using Poco::Geo::CircularGeofence;
    using Poco::Geo::LatLon;

    int main()
    {
        CircularGeofence geofence(LatLon::fromDegrees(48.208180, 16.373820), 500.0);
        LatLon position(LatLon::fromDegrees(48.210000, 16.375000));
        std::cout << (geofence.isInside(position) ? "inside" : "outside") << std::endl;
        return 0;
    }

Both programs link with PocoGeo and PocoFoundation.


!!!Note

<[Angle]> and <[LatLon]> are compared for equality on their degree values, so the usual issues
of comparing floating-point numbers apply. <[LatLon::equals()]> compares two positions with a
delta in degrees.
